---
import DistaDocs from "../../../../components/DistaDocs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const description =
  "The dista command: a generic head, status and status --json, drain, scale --once, timeline and stop; Prometheus metrics on the head and workers; and the task timeline in Chrome's trace format.";

const commands = [
  ["dista head [--port 6390] [--pipeline 2]", "a head for any program, serving several at once"],
  ["dista status [--json]", "the head's workers, drivers and actors; --json, its demand on one line, for an autoscaler"],
  ["dista drain WORKER", "drains the worker at ip:port, exactly as SIGTERM would"],
  ["dista scale --once", "what an autoscaler should do now, from the head's demand"],
  ["dista timeline -o FILE", "every driver's finished tasks, as a Chrome trace"],
  ["dista stop", "shuts the head and its workers down"],
];

const scaleFlags = [
  ["--min", "min_workers", "1"],
  ["--max", "max_workers", "4"],
  ["--per", "tasks_per_worker; 0, the head's pipeline depth", "0"],
  ["--up-after", "up_after, seconds", "5"],
  ["--idle", "idle_before_down, seconds", "60"],
  ["--step", "max_step", "4"],
  ["--pending", "workers started that haven't joined yet", "0"],
  ["--short-since", "the short_since the last decision printed", "0"],
];
---

<DistaDocs href="/s/dista/docs/cli" description={description}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
  <h1 class="section-title">The dista command</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    <code>dista</code> is a head for any Dista program, and a look at a running cluster.
    It depends on Dista's core alone, so your program's own binary stays the driver and
    the worker. Beside it, the head and every worker serve Prometheus metrics, and a
    driver can write its tasks' timeline.
  </p>

  <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#commands">Commands</a>
    <a href="#status">status</a>
    <a href="#scale">scale</a>
    <a href="#metrics">Metrics</a>
    <a href="#timeline">The timeline</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="commands">Commands</h2>
  <p>
    Build it from the repository with <code>pixi run cli</code>, to
    <code>build/dista</code>; the container image has it at
    <code>/opt/dista/bin/dista</code>. Every command but <code>head</code> connects to the
    head as an observer, which owns no objects and isn't counted as a driver. Its head is
    <code>--head host:port</code>, else <code>DISTA_HEAD</code>, else
    <code>localhost:6390</code>.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {commands.map(([k, v]) => (
          <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{v}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <code>--pipeline</code> sets how many tasks the head keeps in flight per worker; see
    <a href="/s/dista/docs/tuning#pipeline">Tuning</a>. <code>stop</code>'s workers exit
    instead of rejoining.
  </p>

  <h2 id="status">status</h2>
  <p>
    <code>dista status</code> prints the head's view: workers, with the program each was
    built from and what it's running or hosting; drivers, with the objects each holds;
    and actors, with their worker, calls and last checkpoint.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>dista status --json</code> is the head's demand, on one line: tasks ready,
    waiting for inputs and running, actors waiting for a worker, the pipeline depth,
    whether there's a checkpoint store, the objects each driver holds
    (<code>driver_objects</code>), and per worker its address, node, tasks in flight,
    seconds idle, bytes of results held, actor and whether it's draining.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scale">scale</h2>
  <p>
    <code>dista scale --once</code> reads the demand and prints
    <code>dista.scale.decide</code>'s decision, a line each: <code>want N</code>,
    <code>start N</code>, <code>drain ADDRESS</code> for each worker to drain, and
    <code>short_since T</code> to pass back as <code>--short-since</code> next time. Its
    flags are the policy's, and what the loop running it knows:
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Flag</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {scaleFlags.map(([k, v, d]) => (
          <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{v}</td><td class="ref">{d}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`dista scale --once --min 0 --max 6 --step 4 --up-after 1 --idle 2 \\
  --pending 0 --short-since 0`} />
  <p>
    <a href="/s/dista/docs/cluster#autoscaling">Autoscaling</a> says how the policy
    decides.
  </p>

  <h2 id="metrics">Metrics</h2>
  <p>
    With <code>DISTA_METRICS_PORT</code> set, the head and every worker serve Prometheus
    metrics at <code>/metrics</code>. The kind manifests turn it on at port 9090, for
    Kubernetes monitoring to scrape. The head's include:
  </p>
  <ul class="docs-list">
    <li>tasks: <code>dista_tasks_submitted_total</code>, <code>dista_tasks_finished_total</code> by outcome, <code>dista_tasks_retried_total</code>, <code>dista_tasks_timed_out_total</code>, and the <code>dista_task_duration_seconds</code> histogram</li>
    <li>demand: <code>dista_queue_depth</code>, <code>dista_tasks_ready</code>, <code>dista_tasks_waiting</code>, <code>dista_tasks_running</code>, <code>dista_workers</code>, <code>dista_workers_idle</code>, <code>dista_workers_draining</code></li>
    <li>memory: <code>dista_objects</code>, <code>dista_head_result_bytes</code>, <code>dista_stored_results</code>, <code>dista_queued_bytes</code>, <code>dista_driver_held_objects_max</code></li>
    <li>backpressure: <code>dista_admission_paused</code>, <code>dista_admission_pauses_total</code>, <code>dista_drivers_behind</code></li>
    <li>actors: <code>dista_actors</code>, <code>dista_actors_waiting</code>, <code>dista_actor_restarts_total</code>, <code>dista_actors_moved_total</code>, <code>dista_checkpoints_total</code>, and <code>dista_results_moved_total</code> for drains</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A worker's metrics count what it runs, stores, spills, copies and adopts:
    <code>dista_worker_tasks_finished_total</code>,
    <code>dista_worker_store_bytes</code>,
    <code>dista_worker_results_spilled_total</code>,
    <code>dista_worker_bytes_copied_total</code>,
    <code>dista_worker_fetch_buffer_bytes</code>,
    <code>dista_worker_results_adopted_total</code> and
    <code>dista_worker_draining</code>, among others.
  </p>

  <h2 id="timeline">The timeline</h2>
  <p>
    The head stamps each task's submission, dispatch and completion, and the worker its
    start and end. <code>rt.timeline(path)</code> writes every finished task, in Chrome's
    trace format, which <a href="https://ui.perfetto.dev" rel="noopener">Perfetto</a> and
    the browser's trace viewer open, and returns how many it wrote. Each worker is a
    track, with a second track for time spent waiting for inputs. The driver can add its
    own work, so a gap between tasks says what it was:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={`var t = rt.clock()
var tasks = scan.plan_files()
rt.span("plan", t)          # from t to now, on the driver's track
...
_ = rt.timeline("trace.json")`} />
  <p>
    <code>dista timeline -o trace.json</code> writes every driver's finished tasks, without
    taking them from the drivers. It names tasks by their wire ID, since a generic head
    doesn't know their names. A head restart loses the spans it held.
  </p>
</DistaDocs>
